  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge to our west will continue to flatten out \nas an upper level Low over Canada continues to pivot northeast, \nwith an associated shortwave trough moving through the Plains.\nMuch of the area should stay dry for the beginning half of the\nday on Wednesday, with some isolated chances (10-25%) of rain\nnorthwest of I-44 during the afternoon, and cloud cover \nincreasing throughout the day. A cold front attached to a \nsurface low over the Great Lakes region is progged to extend \nsouthwest through northern MO and central KS, pushing southeast \ntowards the area throughout the day before stalling out over \ncentral MO. As a result, some isolated to scattered showers and \nstorms will be possible, with the better chances (50-70%) \novernight. Some storms will have the potential to be strong to \nmarginally severe, primarily for areas northwest of Highway 54 \nwhere the better instability looks to set up. As mentioned in\nthe previous discussion, cloud cover and the positioning of the\nfrontal boundary will play a large role in the severe potential.\nIf the frontal lift lines up with the greatest instability, then\nshear would increase, leading to a better chance for severe\nstorms. If this were to occur, a line segment of sorts would be\nprobable, with damaging winds as the main hazard before\ninstability decreases further southeast. As this is still a few\ndays away, more details need to be ironed out before confidence\nincreases in any specifics. \n\nThe front will continue to stall out over central MO Thursday\nbefore retreating back to the north on Friday. This will lead to\nadditional shower and storm chances (30-60%) both days.\n\nOnce this system pushes out, temperatures will begin to\ngradually warm back up, reaching the 90s over the entire area by\nthe end of the weekend and into early next week. We'll be\nkeeping an eye on the Extreme Heat potential, as heat indices\nlook to climb into the 100s for many next week.",
